No, never. The morphology only cares about word forms, never about
their meaning or their syntax.

> http://www.lojban.org/tiki/Morphology%3A+Rules+of+formation
> [[ Any speech generated following these rules will be uniquely
> decomposable by the PEG morphology algorithm. =A0....You must pause in
> the following cases: ... Before any word that starts with a vowel or
> diphthong or y. ]]
>
> It does warn you that you might need to pause before such vowel leading f=
u'ivla.

Semivowels i and u are not considered vowels by the morphology. The
diphthongs are ai, au, ei, and oi.

